00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.760
HelloSign is an application owned by Dropbox&nbsp;
that's used to sign documents. It also integrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.280
with Google Drive so you can ask someone to&nbsp;
sign a document that you store on your Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:26.080
Drive. HelloSign must somehow access files from&nbsp;
your drive. And there are a few ways to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:32.160
integration but let's see how it worked in this&nbsp;
example. Of course, first, you must authenticate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:39.200
to your Google account and authorize HelloSign to&nbsp;
access your files. Then, this iframe pops up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.320
the list of your files. When you finally choose&nbsp;
a file that you would like to sign, the iframe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.880
sends a postMessage to HelloSign which contains&nbsp;
the file ID of the file that you've chosen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.760
Then, you send the request with this file&nbsp;
ID to HelloSign and HelloSign communicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:04.080
with Google Drive API to fetch this file on&nbsp;
the server and then serves you this file.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:11.120
So HelloSign here acts as a proxy. And, of course,&nbsp;
I'm simplifying this process a bit. It takes a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.400
requests but there is nothing interesting or worth&nbsp;
mentioning in between the first and the last one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.800
But let's take a closer look at what actually&nbsp;
HelloSign server does to fetch this file. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.080
the attack was successful, the hunter had to&nbsp;
assume these facts would we now know for certain.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.960
This is Google Drive API endpoint to serve a&nbsp;
file. It contains the file ID which you had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:46.640
send to HelloSign server first. But this endpoint&nbsp;
does not respond directly with a file content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.640
It returns a JSON with metadata about the&nbsp;
file. This metadata contains the downloadUrl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:03:00.960
which is an actual address to the file contents.&nbsp;
So HelloSign must make another request to this URL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.160
to fetch the file which can&nbsp;
eventually be served to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.520
So here HelloSign makes two requests and whenever&nbsp;
you have a functionality where the server makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.680
HTTP requests to the URL which you, at least&nbsp;
partially control, you should be thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:24.400
SSRFs - about the possibility to trick the server&nbsp;
into sending these requests to other locations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.160
And when we look at these two addresses, the&nbsp;
second one doesn't seem to be under our control at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:39.600
all because we can't influence the URL where our&nbsp;
file is stored by Google Drive. The first one is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:46.320
bit better because at least we control the file ID&nbsp;
but unfortunately, it's not possible to trick the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.000
server into sending this request to other host&nbsp;
if you only control the part after the slash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.720
What you can do, however, is&nbsp;
you can try to control the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.560
and parameters of the request. So let's take&nbsp;
a look at the reference of this endpoint to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:09.280
see if there's anything that could give us, for&nbsp;
example, an open redirect. At the first sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.480
it doesn't look like so. None of these&nbsp;
parameters looks to be particularly interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:22.560
However, when we scroll back to the top, we&nbsp;
can see a mention of the alt=media parameter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:29.840
When it's provided, the server doesn't return a&nbsp;
JSON with metadata about the file but it returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:37.280
the file itself. If HelloSign application would&nbsp;
use this parameter, they wouldn't have to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.880
two requests to first fetch the metadata and&nbsp;
then fetch the file but they could fetch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.880
file with a single request. So let's think&nbsp;
about what would happen if we would inject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.840
this parameter into the file ID and&nbsp;
it would not be encoded properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.000
The server would return a whole file&nbsp;
to the first request by HelloSign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.600
But HelloSign would not know about it and it&nbsp;
would try to parse JSON thinking it's metadata.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.640
So all you could do with an image or a pdf is to&nbsp;
force a JSON parsing error - nothing interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:21.200
But if you do this to an actual valid JSON,&nbsp;
then HelloSign will try to parse this JSON,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.800
extract things like the title&nbsp;
and other metadata from the file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.920
and most importantly, it would also extract the&nbsp;
downloadUrl and send a request to this address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:40.080
to then fetch you the response. So this is&nbsp;
a full-read SSRF. You can trick the server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.600
into sending requests to arbitrary locations&nbsp;
and the server gives you the response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:54.000
What's the impact of this? The HelloSign&nbsp;
application is hosted on AWS. You can use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.800
metadata URL which contains AWS credentials&nbsp;
which you can use to log in to the server.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:06.607
From there, you have multiple ways to steal&nbsp;
data of other users of HelloSign application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:15.000
That's why this vulnerability was classified&nbsp;
as critical and rewarded over $17,500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.560
Note the fact that Google&nbsp;
Drive API was not vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:27.280
It behaved exactly like its documentation says.&nbsp;
It's a bug in the integration. There are probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:33.520
thousands of applications that integrate with&nbsp;
Google Drive. A part of them uses the same flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.120
that HelloSign application used and a part of&nbsp;
this surely is still vulnerable to this attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.240
So whenever you see an application that works&nbsp;
like this - that it takes the file ID from you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:52.480
fetches the file on the server - you should&nbsp;
try adding alt=media parameter to the file ID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:58.080
to see what happens. If you see an error,&nbsp;
particularly about JSON parsing, then it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:05.280
good chance that you just found this SSRF. What&nbsp;
you should do then is you should prepare a JSON&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:11.280
that looks like a metadata about the file. For&nbsp;
that, refer to Google Drive API documentation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

